    {
      "id": "cmsfkfv9c001v04jooywp1ptm",
      "term": "What are non-recurring items? What do we generally do with them?",
      "setTitle": "Accounting - \"Talking\"",
      "klps": [
        {
          "text": "Non-recurring items are one-off income or expense items that don't reflect the run-rate performance of the business",
          "kind": "definition"
        },
        {
          "text": "Typical examples are restructuring charges, inventory write-downs, impairments, and gains or losses on asset sales",
          "kind": "example"
        },
        {
          "text": "Non-recurring items are added back when comparing companies because they are not part of core operations",
          "kind": "causal"
        },
        {
          "text": "The add-back gets you to earnings the business can be expected to generate going forward",
          "kind": "causal"
        },
        {
          "text": "Caveat: these are still real cash costs, and some companies take 'one-off' charges repeatedly, so don't ignore them entirely",
          "kind": "condition"
        },
        {
          "text": "Adjustments should be symmetric — strip out unusually large one-off gains, not just charges",
          "kind": "contrast"
        }
      ],
